A||||5||ERIC STEWART||NITROS OXIDE ||||
Z||000000||ERIC STEWART||09-06-99||08:19 PM||||     I am looking for some advise on instaling a nitros oxide injection kit in my 1986 & 1/2 fiero GT.  The boys at NOS tell me I don't need to make any modifications to my stock 2.8.  The car has less than 2,000 miles on it, and I don't want to harm the engine (but I do want it to be more powerful).  I welcome any comments or concerns from fellow Fiero owners who have installed similiar nitros systems in their own cars.   
Z||000001||Dale||09-06-99||08:39 PM||||dont do a big system!!  Those are cast pistons in their.  They melt easy.
Z||000002||Shaun41178||09-08-1999||09:06 AM||||I have installed an NOS kit on my Fiero with good results.  I am running a 70 shot of nitrous.  I have had the kit for over a year now and everything is still holding togethor after plenty of 1/4 mile dashes and a street race every once in a while.  I think that adding nitrous to my car was the best money I could've spent to get my car faster.  My best time with nitrous is a 14.404.  You can email with any questions you have regarding the purchasing or anything else in regards to nitrous.  You can see my install of my nitrous system at <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178</A> <P>If used wisely nitrous oxide will not do severe damage.  Yes it can happen but when used in moderate to conservative amounts it is completly safe as long as everything was installed properly and directions are followed to the letter.<P>You should be able to run all day on a 50 shot of nitrous and a 70 should give no problems as well.  anything over that and you can be asking for trouble.  The stock 2.8 should be able to hold a 100 shot of nitrous but i don't know for how long.  That is pushing a stock 2.8 really good.  Like i said I have had no problems for a year now with running a 70 shot.||||reg||
Z||000003||mwbackus||09-08-1999||05:57 PM||||Heh Shaun,<BR>   I was interested in the operating cost of your system.  Approximatly how much does it cost to fill the bottle (size?) and how many runs would you get with a 50 or 70 hp shot?<P>Mike||||reg||
Z||000004||Shaun41178||09-09-1999||02:13 AM||||mwbackus:  The bottle provided with the kit holds 10 pounds of nitrous oxide.  Price here in toledo area ranges from $3-$3.50 per pound.  So the most it could cost to fill the bottle will be around $35 or so dollars.  However performance starts to drop off once the bottle starts to get low cause the bottle pressure drops off as well.  I have only paid $21 at the most to have my bottle filled up.  This is because I never let it get completely empty.<P>As far as how many runs I can get out of a full bottle it is quite a few.  I have never gone to the track and emptied a whole bottle of nitrous but a 50-70 shot should get you 12-15 full qtr mile passes before the bottle is tapped out.  It lasts a long time using such conservative amounts.  When out street racing(please no speaches people) I probably only go through a puond or two all night.  This probably includes like 2-3 maybe even 4 races.<P>Nitrousworks makes a kit sold through Jegs for $439 and is an adjustable unit even though the mag says it is not.  A pretty good deal if you ask me. However the Nitrous works bottle does not come filled so add around $35 to have it filled.  NOS kit comes with the bottle full but costs more too.  Both kits are good.||||reg||
Z||000005||ERIC STEWART||09-12-1999||09:18 PM||||     Thanks for the responses everyone.  I'll let you know how it turns out!||||reg||1
